15. TRAVERSAL VALIDATION — NO FALSE APPROVALS
============================================================

The current validator uses simplified maximum jump
distance and height comparisons.

It is not sufficient.

It also treats the entire Drifter patrol envelope
as a permanently walkable surface.

A temporary Bloom is also treated too optimistically.

Build a more rigorous deterministic traversal validator.

Use actual movement parameters:

- Horizontal movement speed.
- Ground acceleration.
- Air acceleration.
- Ground deceleration.
- Gravity.
- Jump impulse.
- Variable jump height.
- Collision body dimensions.
- Jump buffering.
- Coyote time.
- Landing geometry.

SIMULATION:

Simulate player trajectories with small deterministic
time increments.

Account for actual collision geometry and realistic
takeoff and landing positions.

Test multiple jump-hold durations and horizontal
input sequences.

A theoretical maximum is not a guaranteed practical jump.

For moving platforms:

- Simulate boarding time.
- Simulate platform location.
- Simulate passenger movement.
- Simulate departure timing.
- Validate the next landing.

For Bloom platforms:

- Validate activation distance.
- Validate Wake timing.
- Validate remaining active duration.
- Validate boarding and departure.
- Validate the following landing.

For Root Bridges:

- Validate that the interaction can be reached.
- Validate the bridge position.
- Validate the traversal route after activation.

Build a route graph:

NODES:
- Static platforms.
- Temporary Bloom platforms.
- Moving Drifters.
- Root bridges.
- Checkpoints.
- Level exits.

EDGES:
- Valid jumps.
- Valid timed transitions.
- Moving-platform transfers.
- Wake-dependent traversal.

Prove that a valid route from spawn to the goal exists.

A successful graph must not rely on a fictional
permanently stationary Drifter or Bloom.

Run at least 50 deterministic seeds per difficulty
during generator QA.

Record:
- Validation result.
- Failed segments.
- Maximum gap.
- Height changes.
- Required timing.
- Route connectivity.

Regenerate failed layouts.

Use a known safe fallback if repeated generation fails.

Never fake a validation success.

